IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `oapa-nouns`

-- Offences against the Person Act 1861 -- LAYER 1: MAKING GUNPOWDER TO COMMIT OFFENCES,
-- AND SEARCHING FOR THE SAME (sections 64 and 65).
--
-- Section 64 refers to "any of the felonies in this Act mentioned".  The text calls an
-- offence a felony in its own section ("shall be guilty of felony"), so which offences
-- are felonies is worked out from the sections, not asked: each section module has a
-- rule `s N: what the text calls the offence`, and `oapa-table.l4` gathers them.  This
-- module cannot import that table (the table imports this module for section 64's own
-- classification), so the rule takes the classification of the offence intended as a
-- fact of type Classification and tests it for `felony`.
--
-- Section 65 gives a justice of the peace a power to issue a search warrant.  It is an
-- official's power; what it means to a person is that their premises, carriage or
-- vessel can be searched and what is found seized.  The power is encoded for that.

§ `Making Gunpowder to commit Offences, and searching for the same`

§§ `Section 64: making or having gunpowder, &c., with intent to commit any felony against this Act`

-- src:106 | 64 Making or having gunpowder, &c., with intent to commit any felony against this Act.
-- src:107 | Whosoever shall knowingly have in his possession, or make or manufacture, any gunpowder, explosive substance, or any dangerous or noxious thing, or any machine, engine, instrument, or thing, with intent by means thereof to commit, or for the purpose of enabling any other person to commit, any of the felonies in this Act mentioned shall be guilty of a misdemeanor, and being convicted thereof shall be liable, at the discretion of the court, to be imprisoned for any term not exceeding two years, . . .
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 64 (src:106-107)
-- "knowingly have in his possession, or make or manufacture, any gunpowder, explosive
-- substance, or any dangerous or noxious thing, or any machine, engine, instrument, or
-- thing, with intent by means thereof to commit, or for the purpose of enabling any
-- other person to commit, any of the felonies in this Act mentioned".  The thing can
-- be any of that list.  The intent is the person's own, or to enable another.
-- `how the text classifies the offence intended` is `felony` only for the offences the
-- text itself calls felonies (see `oapa-table.l4`).
DECLARE `Conduct under section 64` HAS
    `knowingly had in possession, or made or manufactured, gunpowder, an explosive substance, a dangerous or noxious thing, or a machine, engine, instrument or thing` IS A BOOLEAN
    `intended by means of it to commit an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `made it for the purpose of enabling another person to commit an offence` IS A BOOLEAN
    `how the text classifies the offence intended` IS A Classification

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 64 (src:107)
GIVEN c IS A `Conduct under section 64`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 64: making or having gunpowder or a dangerous thing to commit a felony of this Act` c MEANS
        c's `knowingly had in possession, or made or manufactured, gunpowder, an explosive substance, a dangerous or noxious thing, or a machine, engine, instrument or thing`
    AND (   c's `intended by means of it to commit an offence`
         OR c's `made it for the purpose of enabling another person to commit an offence`)
    AND c's `how the text classifies the offence intended` EQUALS felony

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 64 (src:107)
GIVETH A Classification
`s 64: what the text calls the offence` MEANS misdemeanor

-- "liable, at the discretion of the court, to be imprisoned for any term not exceeding
-- two years": 24 months.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 64 (src:107)
GIVETH A LIST OF Sentence
`s 64: the sentences the text states` MEANS
    LIST `imprisonment` (`for a term not exceeding` 24)

§§ `Section 65: justices may issue warrants for searching houses, &c. in which explosive substances are suspected to be made for the purpose of committing felonies against this Act`

-- src:108 | 65 Justices may issue warrants for searching houses, &c. in which explosive substances are suspected to be made for the purpose of committing felonies against this Act.
-- src:109 | Where any such gunpowder, or other explosive, dangerous, or noxious substance or thing, or any such machine, engine, instrument or thing, is suspected to be made, kept, or carried for the purpose of being used in committing any of the felonies in this Act mentioned, a justice of the peace, upon reasonable cause assigned upon oath by any person, may issue a warrant . . . for searching, . . . , any house, mill, magazine, storehouse, warehouse, shop, cellar, yard, wharf, or other place, or any carriage, waggon, cart, ship, boat, or vessel, in which the same is suspected to be made, kept, or carried for such purpose as herein-before mentioned; and every person acting in the execution of any such warrant shall have, for seizing, removing to proper places, and detaining all such gunpowder, explosive, dangerous, or noxious substances, machines, engines, instruments, or things, found upon such search, which he shall have good cause to suspect to be intended to be used in committing any such offences, and the barrels, packages, cases and other receptacles in which the same shall be, the same powers and protections which are given to persons searching for unlawful quantities of gunpowder under the warrant of a justice by the Act passed in the session holden in the twenty-third and twenty-fourth years of the reign of Her present Majesty, chapter one hundred and thirty-nine, intituled, “An Act to amend the law concerning the making, keeping, and carriage of gunpowder and compositions of an explosive nature, and concerning the manufacture, sale, and use of fireworks.”
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 65 (src:108-109)
-- The power: "Where any such gunpowder, or other explosive, dangerous, or noxious
-- substance or thing, or any such machine, engine, instrument or thing, is suspected to
-- be made, kept, or carried for the purpose of being used in committing any of the
-- felonies in this Act mentioned, a justice of the peace, upon reasonable cause
-- assigned upon oath by any person, may issue a warrant . . . for searching, . . . ,
-- any house, mill, magazine, storehouse, warehouse, shop, cellar, yard, wharf, or other
-- place, or any carriage, waggon, cart, ship, boat, or vessel, in which the same is
-- suspected to be made, kept, or carried for such purpose".
-- Whether the cause assigned is "reasonable" is the justice's judgment, taken as a fact
-- about the application ("if the justice accepts that the cause is reasonable").
DECLARE `Search under section 65` HAS
    `such a thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried for the purpose of being used in committing a felony mentioned in the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
    `cause was assigned upon oath by a person` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the justice of the peace accepts that the cause assigned is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the place is a house, mill, magazine, storehouse, warehouse, shop, cellar, yard, wharf, or other place, or a carriage, waggon, cart, ship, boat or vessel, in which the thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the thing was found upon the search` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person executing the warrant has good cause to suspect it to be intended to be used in committing such a felony` IS A BOOLEAN

@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 65 (src:109)
GIVEN s IS A `Search under section 65`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 65: a justice of the peace may issue a warrant to search the place` s MEANS
        s's `such a thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried for the purpose of being used in committing a felony mentioned in the Act`
    AND s's `cause was assigned upon oath by a person`
    AND s's `the justice of the peace accepts that the cause assigned is reasonable`
    AND s's `the place is a house, mill, magazine, storehouse, warehouse, shop, cellar, yard, wharf, or other place, or a carriage, waggon, cart, ship, boat or vessel, in which the thing is suspected to be made, kept or carried`

-- "every person acting in the execution of any such warrant shall have, for seizing,
-- removing to proper places, and detaining all such gunpowder, explosive, dangerous, or
-- noxious substances, machines, engines, instruments, or things, found upon such
-- search, which he shall have good cause to suspect to be intended to be used in
-- committing any such offences, and the barrels, packages, cases and other receptacles
-- in which the same shall be, the same powers and protections which are given to
-- persons searching for unlawful quantities of gunpowder under the warrant of a justice
-- by the Act passed in the session holden in the twenty-third and twenty-fourth years
-- of the reign of Her present Majesty, chapter one hundred and thirty-nine".  What those
-- powers and protections ARE is in that other Act, which is not an input here, so the
-- encoding states only that the power to seize, remove and detain exists.
@ref Offences against the Person Act 1861 s 65 (src:109)
GIVEN s IS A `Search under section 65`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 65: the thing and its receptacles may be seized, removed and detained` s MEANS
        `s 65: a justice of the peace may issue a warrant to search the place` s
    AND s's `the thing was found upon the search`
    AND s's `the person executing the warrant has good cause to suspect it to be intended to be used in committing such a felony`
